From 0bd9e3a1144862647db3cc17c8f9f19b77f6730c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ebg-ant <296364998+ebg-ant@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 00:55:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat: carry a window with the keyboard (F59)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

⌃⌥⌘Space picks the focused window up, the arrows walk a preview through every
place a drag could choose, ↩ or Space puts it down, ⎋ puts everything back. The
view shortcuts work meanwhile; ⌃⌥⌘[ and ⌃⌥⌘] choose among the places on another
display. One step to undo.

CarryMap (ArborCore) lists the drop targets in view and links them by sending an
imaginary pointer through DropResolver; the drop is finishDrop, as for a drag.

While a window is carried Arbor holds plain keys. Every way out gives them back,
also the keyboard going to a sheet or the wallpaper, a press of the mouse, no
display left, and half a minute without a key, which only a key begins again.
Only ⎋, putting down in place and the half minute put the view back: whatever
else ends a carry lays out for itself, and a window moves once. Put down where
it is, a window goes through no drop: a floating window's frame is its person's.

The harness, the chaos lab and the stand-in Mac go by the temporary shortcuts
the app was told of, not by what the controller would say if asked.

Also: lint-imports.py refuses geometry types as dictionary keys or set elements,
and doctor names each sign a keyboard layout cannot type once.
