+### Put a window exactly there, without the mouse
+
+**⌃⌥⌘Space** picks up the window you are typing in. A preview shows where it would land, starting where it is. The **arrow keys** walk the preview through every place a drag could choose: toward a window they offer its near edge, then its far edge, then
+what lies beyond, and ↑ and ↓ its top and bottom. **↩** or Space puts the window down; **⎋** puts everything back. So "Slack under Terminal" is ⌃⌥⌘Space → ↓ ↩.
+The view shortcuts still work while you carry: ⌃⌥2, then ↩, takes the window into the space you saved as view 2; ⌃⌥↓ looks closer at the place the preview is on. ⌃⌥⌘] carries it to the next display. The Desktop is a place like any other: put a docked window down there and it floats, at the size it had before it was docked.
+No window changes its place until you put this one down; only the view moves (a window that fills the view is shown with its surroundings when you pick it up). Put down where it was, by ⎋, by ⌃⌥⌘Space again or by nothing at all for half a minute, and everything is as it was, the view included. A window that floats is not touched at all.
+Do anything else (another shortcut, a click anywhere, ⌘Tab, a dialog that opens) and the carry is over at once and the arrows, ↩, Space and ⎋ are your app's again. The view then stays as it is, so that nothing moves twice; ⌃⌥[ goes back.
+
