+## Shipped (2026-10-14, `feat/F59-carry`)
+
+Everything above, against simulated windows. A carry is one step to undo ("Undo Carry"); one that is cancelled, or put down where it was, leaves none.
+
+- **The arrows go by an imaginary pointer, not by the previews' middles.** The order the spec gives first ("the nearest target whose preview lies that way")
+  leaves the edges of the view unreachable: their previews are large and their middles lie among the others (201 of 3000 random
+  arrangements had a choice no key reached). The order it gives second ("as a pointer crossing it would") is now the rule itself: the pointer
+  travels through `DropResolver.resolve`.
+- Two targets count as one only if the whole arrangement would look the same, not just the carried window's frame.
+- `DropResolver.targets(…)` became `CarryMap`, which also holds the previews and the links between choices.
+- It ends in F09's `finishDrop(_:on:)`, and so in `Drop.apply(to:)`, the one place where a drop changes workspaces.
+- Also: `carry` again puts down; ⌃⌥⌘[ and ⌃⌥⌘] pick up as well; a tab can be put beside the rest of its group; `Carry…` in the Dock
+  Focused Window menu; half a minute without a key cancels.
+- **Differs from "any other command puts everything back, view included":** only ⎋, putting down in place and the half minute put the view back.
+  Whatever else ends a carry leaves the view as it is (⌃⌥[ goes back), because something that lays out for itself follows, and a window moves
+  once. Reasons and the one exception are in collab/QUESTIONS.md.
+- **What a carry does to a window that floats** (its frame is its person's, Q20): picked up and put down where it is, by any ending, it is not asked to move
+  and nothing Arbor remembers of it changes; it starts at its own place also with the Desktop out of view; carried out of the Desktop's view and back it
+  makes the two moves every floating window makes, to the point where it was; carried to another display's Desktop it keeps its size and moves once. The
+  others that float are not touched while the preview walks. What a **drop** does to them is what a dragged drop does.
+- A docked window dropped on the Desktop has the size it floated at, moves once, and glides there with the others.
+- Adversarial readings before it landed found three ways on which the plain keys stayed with Arbor (the keyboard going to a sheet or the wallpaper; any
+  layout putting off the half minute; no display left), and that with other bands than the default (`screenEdgeBand`, `windowEdgeFraction`) an arrow could lead from a
+  choice to itself and some choices be out of reach. Each has a test now, in `CarryEndingsTests.swift` and `CarryTests.swift`; the chaos lab has the rules that
+  would have found the first three (the window in hand has the keyboard; the app has been told what holds).
+
+## Not shipped
+
+- **Real windows: never tried, with real keys or without.** `scripts/mac-shell/check-F59.sh` is written and has never run on a Mac (M1545). Above all nobody has seen
+  that plain keys (arrows, ↩, Space, ⎋) can be held for the length of a carry and reach Arbor, and that they go back to the app after
+  **every** ending. A peek's temporary keys all come with ⌃⌥; these come with nothing. The script tries a drop, ⎋, a click in another window and the same key
+  again, and after each presses all seven and reads in the log whether Arbor heard one; not the other endings.
+- **A right-click does not end a carry.** "A press of the mouse" reaches the controller for the left button only (`AXWindowSystem`'s monitor matches `.leftMouseDown`), and a context menu
+  reports nothing. Until the half minute is over the arrows, ↩ and ⎋ steer the carry and not the menu. The cure is three lines in a macOS-only file (match `.rightMouseDown` and
+  `.otherMouseDown` too, and call `pointerPressed()` for them); it belongs with the first run of the script on a Mac, with a step that right-clicks.
+- Only a Mac can say what a **held** key does: ↩, Space and ⎋ are given back when they go down, so their repeats may reach the app (newlines in a chat). And Spotlight
+  reports nothing when it takes the keyboard: there only the half minute ends a carry.
+- Not known either (M1546, M1547): whether `Carry…` chosen from the menu outlives the menu closing, how the preview looks on a second display, what a held arrow's
+  repeat does, and how long making the map takes on a Mac.
+- How a tile is arranged inside cannot be chosen from outside: look closer first; ⌃⌥↓ works while carrying (K1481).
+- The other windows do not make way while the preview walks, as they do in a drag with live reflow: only the preview moves (K1482).
+- With the window in hand out of view, another command still moves windows twice: the view goes back first, because that window has the keyboard.
+- Making the map takes a fifth of a second for 12 windows on a wide display in a debug build, half a second on 5120 × 2880; it is made again at every layout of the
+  display during a carry. Not measured in a release build or on a Mac.
+- A line of its own on the display (K1480). Pressed with keys, each step's words do show there ("Below Terminal"), but only by F43's rule for a key that
+  changed nothing anyone can see; no test of F59's pins that.
